DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The iissccssiiccttll utility is used to configure the iSCSI initiator.

     The following options are available:

     ----lliibbxxoo            Generate output via libxo(3) in a selection of differ‐
                        ent human and machine readable formats.  See
                        xo_parse_args(3) for details on command line argu‐
                        ments.

     --AA                 Add session.

     --MM                 Modify session.

     --RR                 Remove session.

     --LL                 List sessions.

     --aa                 When adding, add all sessions defined in the configu‐
                        ration file.  When removing, remove all currently es‐
                        tablished sessions.

     --cc _p_a_t_h            Path to the configuration file.  The default is
                        _/_e_t_c_/_i_s_c_s_i_._c_o_n_f.

     --dd _d_i_s_c_o_v_e_r_y_-_h_o_s_t  Target host name or address used for SendTargets dis‐
                        covery.  When used, it will add a temporary discovery
                        session.  After discovery is done, sessions will be
                        added for each discovered target, and the temporary
                        discovery session will be removed.

     --ee oonn|ooffff          Enable or disable the session.  This is ignored for
                        discovery sessions, but gets passed down to normal
                        sessions they add.

     --ii _s_e_s_s_i_o_n_-_i_d      Session ID, as displayed by iissccssiiccttll --vv.

     --nn _n_i_c_k_n_a_m_e        The _n_i_c_k_n_a_m_e of a session defined in the configuration
                        file.

     --pp _p_o_r_t_a_l          Target portal — host name or address — for statically
                        defined targets.

     --rr                 Use iSER (iSCSI over RDMA) instead of plain iSCSI over
                        TCP/IP.

     --ss _s_e_c_r_e_t          CHAP secret.

     --tt _t_a_r_g_e_t          Target name.

     --uu _u_s_e_r            CHAP login.

     --vv                 Verbose mode.

     --ww _t_i_m_e_o_u_t         Instead of returning immediately, wait up to _t_i_m_e_o_u_t
                        seconds until all configured sessions are successfully
                        established.

     Certain parameters are necessary when adding a session.  One can specify
     these either via command line (using the --tt, --pp, --uu, and --ss options), or
     configuration file (using the --aa or --nn options).  Some functionality -
     for example mutual CHAP - is available only via configuration file.

     Since connecting to the target is performed in background, non-zero exit
     status does not mean that the session was successfully established.  Use
     either iissccssiiccttll --LL to check the connection status, or the --ww flag to wait
     for session establishment.

     Note that in order for the iSCSI initiator to be able to connect to a
     target, the iscsid(8) daemon must be running.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/iscsi.conf  iSCSI initiator configuration file.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The iissccssiiccttll utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
